peer_feedback_assignment_1:
  issue_number: 45
  issue_owner: felisita101
  issue_ok: no
  issue_status: "Could not access the following files:\n - https://felisita101.github.io/STA9750-2026-SPRING/site_libs/bootstrap/bootstrap-b88270fc82d54e1cef088be51ab81bec.min.css\n
    - https://felisita101.github.io/STA9750-2026-SPRING/mp02_files/figure-html/unnamed-chunk-22-1.png\n
    - https://felisita101.github.io/STA9750-2026-SPRING/mp02_files/figure-html/unnamed-chunk-23-1.png\n
    - https://felisita101.github.io/STA9750-2026-SPRING/mp02_files/figure-html/unnamed-chunk-24-1.png\n
    - https://felisita101.github.io/STA9750-2026-SPRING/mp02_files/figure-html/unnamed-chunk-25-1.png\n
    - https://felisita101.github.io/STA9750-2026-SPRING/mp02_files/figure-html/unnamed-chunk-26-1.png\n
    - https://felisita101.github.io/STA9750-2026-SPRING/mp02_files/figure-html/unnamed-chunk-27-1.png\n
    - https://felisita101.github.io/STA9750-2026-SPRING/mp02_files/figure-html/unnamed-chunk-28-1.png\nPlease
    make sure to `git add` the corresponding files from your\n`docs` repository. Once
    you have done this, make a new commit and\n`git push` to update your GitHub repository.
    You do not need to open\na new issue or re-post here (though adding a new comment
    won't hurt)\n- simply pushing the updated and corrected files will automatically
    \npropogate through to this issue thread."
  overall_comments:
    positive: the gt tables were well built.
    negative: the report needs an actual narrative this looks like draft
    advice: Go through each section and replace the hashtag comments with actual prose
      and write a sentence explaining what you found and why it matters.
  scores:
    Written Communication:
      score: 3.0
      comment: It reads like a draft script, not a report. Findings are in hashtag
        commets and not actual sentences. No narrative or story or intro.
    Project Skeleton:
      score: 5.0
      comment: Most tasks are attempted and some are done well. The submission cuts
        off before Task 6 is complete though, and the recurring many-to-many join
        warnings suggest the data pipeline has issues that could affect the results.
    Tables & Document Presentation:
      score: 6.0
      comment: The gt tables that are there are actually solid — spanners, source
        notes, formatted numbers. But a lot of results are just printed as raw tibble
        output instead of being put in a proper table.
    Data Visualization:
      score: 4.0
      comment: Two charts present, both above default quality with custom colors.
        But legends are crowded, some axis labels are vague, and the overall polish
        isn't quite there yet.
    Exploratory Data Analysis:
      score: 6.0
      comment: No glimpse() or skim() on the ATUS data, and no broader exploration
        of the dataset. Everything goes straight to answering specific questions without
        stepping back to understand the data first.
    Code Quality:
      score: 5.0
      comment: The many-to-many join warnings are the main issues, they should have
        been caught and fixed before submission. No code folding either, so warnings
        and raw output are all over the rendered document.
    Data Preparation:
      score: 10.0
      comment: DEFAULT SCORE
    Analysis and Findings:
      score: 4.0
      comment: Some genuinely interesting ideas (retired vs. full-time lawn care,
        transit mode activities) but most findings have no written interpretation.
        The join issue also raises questions about whether the weighted estimates
        are accurate.
    Extra Credit:
      score: 0.0
      comment: no extra credit attempted
  mp_number: 2
peer_feedback_assignment_2:
  issue_number: 64
  issue_owner: janielave
  issue_ok: yes
  issue_status: OK
  overall_comments:
    positive: EDA section is very solid, the running glimse and summary
    negative: Visualization is completely missing
    advice: add at least two ggplot charts
  scores:
    Written Communication:
      score: 7.0
      comment: There's an intro and findings are written in sentences, which is good.
        But one sentence per finding with no context or interpretation. The report
        feels like a checklist rather than an analysis. No market research deliverable
        is visible either.
    Project Skeleton:
      score: 5.0
      comment: Tasks 2, 4, and 5 are all attempted. Some findings are off, average
        sleep of 4 hours is clearly wrong (should be ~8.5), and the childcare estimate
        of 0.51 hours is suspiciously low. These suggest weighting issues in the join
        pipeline. Task 6 (market research profiles) doesn't appear to be present.
    Tables & Document Presentation:
      score: 4.0
      comment: Tables exist but need work. Raw column names throughout, no number
        formatting, and the high-earner table is a 57-row dump with NA groups mixed
        in. That's exactly what the rubric flags as a data dump.
    Data Visualization:
      score: 3.0
      comment: No visualizations visible in the submission. The assignment specifically
        warns this will be penalized severely.
    Exploratory Data Analysis:
      score: 10.0
      comment: DEFAULT SCORE
    Code Quality:
      score: 5.0
      comment: The many-to-many join warning appears multiple times and was never
        fixed. The sleep and childcare calculations use match() for weighting which
        is error-prone and likely why those numbers are so far off.
    Data Preparation:
      score: 10.0
      comment: DEFAULT SCORE
    Analysis and Findings:
      score: 4.0
      comment: 4 hours of sleep and 0.51 hours of childcare are clearly wrong. The
        other findings are directionally reasonable but have no written interpretation
        behind them, and the market research deliverable is missing.
    Extra Credit:
      score: 0.0
      comment: no extra credit
  mp_number: 2
peer_feedback_assignment_3:
  issue_number: 66
  issue_owner: asarker00
  issue_ok: no
  issue_status: |-
    Could not access the following files:
     - https://asarker00.github.io/STA9750-2026-SPRING/@import
  overall_comments:
    positive: product framing is outstanding
    negative: adding interactivity or animation would make visualisation top tier,
      but nothing missing to be honest
    advice: Try using the plotly package to wrap one of the existing ggplot charts
      with ggplotly()
  scores:
    Written Communication:
      score: 9.0
      comment: The intro is compelling and catches readers attention, it also frames
        the whole analysis around a product
    Project Skeleton:
      score: 10.0
      comment: All tasks are present and clearly labeled. Tasks 2 through 6 are all
        accounted for, the market research deliverable covers three distinct demographic
        profiles, and the join logic is explicitly documented with thoughtful design
        decisions. Nothing is missing.
    Tables & Document Presentation:
      score: 9.0
      comment: The data inventory table at the top with styling, bold headers, and
        source notes sets the tone immediately. Everything appears polished and consistent.
        The only thing that might push it to a 10 would be color formatting or embedded
        sparklines in the tables.
    Data Visualization:
      score: 8.0
      comment: Code folding is used well throughout and the graphical explorations
        section exists. The charts appear above exploratory quality with proper labeling.
        To reach a 9 or 10 would require more advanced features like interactivity
        or animation.
    Exploratory Data Analysis:
      score: 10.0
      comment: DEFAULT SCORE
    Code Quality:
      score: 10.0
      comment: The function-level documentation with explicit notes about where variables
        live (e.g. "TUMONTH is on resp, NOT on act") is genuinely excellent. This
        is the kind of code you'd actually want to hand off to a colleague. Fully
        idiomatic tidyverse with no warnings.
    Data Preparation:
      score: 10.0
      comment: DEFAULT SCORE
    Analysis and Findings:
      score: 8.0
      comment: Three well-defined demographic personas with data-grounded profiles
        is exactly what the assignment asks for. The findings are believable and actionable.
        The one thing holding it back from a 10 is that I couldn't confirm the most
        subtle insights from the market research section in what was fetched.
    Extra Credit:
      score: 0.0
      comment: none
  mp_number: 2
evaluator: dhwanikan

